  "intro": "An improper integral has an infinite limit or an integrand that blows up inside the interval. Evaluate it as a limit: $\\int_a^\\infty f\\,dx=\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\int_a^t f\\,dx$, and for a vertical asymptote at $b$, $\\int_a^b f\\,dx=\\lim_{t\\to b^-}\\int_a^t f\\,dx$. If the limit is a finite number the integral converges; otherwise it diverges. The p-test: $\\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{x^p}\\,dx$ converges exactly when $p>1$.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^2}\\,dx$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Rewrite as a limit: $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\int_1^t x^{-2}\\,dx$.",
          "Antiderivative: $\\left[-\\frac{1}{x}\\right]_1^t=-\\frac{1}{t}+1$.",
          "Take the limit: $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\left(1-\\frac{1}{t}\\right)=1$.",
          "The integral converges to $1$."
        ],
        "answer": "Converges to $1$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Determine whether $\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x}\\,dx$ converges.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Limit form: $\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\int_1^t \\frac{1}{x}\\,dx=\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}[\\ln x]_1^t=\\lim_{t\\to\\infty}\\ln t$.",
          "Since $\\ln t\\to\\infty$, the limit is infinite.",
          "The integral diverges (consistent with the p-test, $p=1$)."
        ],
        "answer": "Diverges"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Evaluate $\\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,dx$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "The integrand blows up at $x=0$, so use $\\lim_{t\\to 0^+}\\int_t^1 x^{-1/2}\\,dx$.",
          "Antiderivative: $\\left[2\\sqrt{x}\\right]_t^1=2-2\\sqrt{t}$.",
          "Take the limit: $\\lim_{t\\to 0^+}(2-2\\sqrt{t})=2$.",
          "The integral converges to $2$."
        ],
        "answer": "Converges to $2$"
      }
    },
